{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:b68fb615b2be69581bd8e185531bd1570cbd52225acbaa1ffc00df6ad0d1fa2a","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.75},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.75},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.65}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR ADOPTED: the Historian's 0.8 / 1.9 / 2.9.\nPPP WEIGHT, RESOLVED FIRST BECAUSE IT DOMINATES. The three Wave-1 seats disagree materially: Fermi 0.38 China, Historian 0.33, Scout 0.32. Fermi's 0.365-0.38 rests on OECD-38 PPP GDP of ~$73T against China ~$42T; that OECD figure is too low. Rebuilt: world PPP GDP ~$195T, China ~19.5% = ~$38T; OECD-38 sums to ~41% (US 14.8, Japan 3.2, Germany 3.0, UK 2.2, France 2.1, Turkey 2.0, Italy 1.8, Mexico 1.8, Korea 1.7, Spain 1.4, Canada 1.3, Poland 0.9, Australia 0.9, rest ~4) = ~$80T. China share of the pair = 38/118 = 0.322 at the 2026 start, drifting to ~0.34-0.35 by the 2034 midpoint. I use 0.335 / 0.665. This is a ratio and so deflator-base invariant; I ADOPT the Fermi seat's reasoning that a weight is identified while rejecting its level.\nTHE HEADLINE PRODUCT: 0.335 x (-2.7pp China aging drag) = -0.90pp of coupled growth from Chinese demographics alone; 0.665 x (-0.4pp OECD per-capita drag) = -0.27pp; total -1.17pp/yr. Against that, the probability-weighted AI TFP lift computed off AI-Q6's own regime means (0.162 x 0.75 + 0.472 x 0.35 + 0.365 x 0.07) = +0.31pp, net about +0.25pp after the aging-demand-pull plus (+0.05), fiscal-crowding-out minus (-0.05) and incremental Baumol reallocation (-0.06) adjustments. The offset is roughly a fifth of the drag. No Bayesian update changes this; it is the structure.\nLEGS: OECD-38 = 0.80pp locked aggregate baseline (0.6% per capita + ~0.2% population) + 0.25pp net AI + 0.07pp net capital services (gross +0.14 halved for the AI-Q4 digestion phase landing inside the window) - 0.08pp damages/energy-cost = 1.04%; cross-checked by composition at 1.11%; I take 1.15%, a touch above Fermi, because the Scout's 1.25% carries the observed 2026 run-rate correctly. China = 2.8%.\nCOUPLED P50 = 0.665 x 1.15 + 0.335 x 2.80 = 0.765 + 0.938 = 1.70%, plus ~0.05pp of within-window weight drift = 1.75%.\nUPDATES, WITH LRs.\n(1) IMF Oct-2026 WEO: global 3.1% in 2026 (raised 0.1pp) rebounding to 3.4% in 2027, US \"expected to remain at 2.3 percent\", EU subdued; no Oct-2026-edition China or AE figure was verifiable, and the 4.5% China number in circulation is from the January-2026 update. Implied bloc run-rate ~2.6%. LR for \"2026-27 run-rate 2.6%\" | (2032-36 >= 2.2%) vs (< 2.2%) is about 1.3 — the autocorrelation of five-year growth windows at a six-year lag is weak for aggregates this large. BELOW 2:1, DISCOUNTED to 1.15. Worth +0.05pp on P50 and nothing else.\n(2) PHYSICAL BOTTLENECK, the strongest signal: generator step-up transformer lead times surpassing 160 weeks by Q1 2026 against a 143-week 2024 average; gas turbine lead times stretched to five years after 43 GW of 2025 orders, the strongest in two decades; lead-time-to-power 36 months in Pittsburgh to 84 months in Columbus; ERCOT large-load queue ~410 GW in April 2026 (caveat preserved verbatim in substance: developers hedge across queues, so the figure is padded with duplicates and overstates real load). LR: P(these lead times | the 2032-36 realized TFP contribution is capped below ~+0.35pp) / P(same | uncapped) = about 2.5. ABOVE 2:1, KEPT. The logic is a date, not a theory: capacity operational in 2032-36 must be queued by 2029-32, so the marginal project in the window is equipment-constrained today. Worth -0.08pp on P50 and, more importantly, a hard cut to P90.\n(3) THE SIMULATION, treated as ONE piece of evidence with its own LR, not as prior and not as fact. The Fermi seat's adapter returns 2.95% (baseline and central-path), 3.03% (ai-energy-boom), 3.68% (tech-breakthrough), 2.30%/2.29% (tech-stagnation/plateau), 1.67% (debt-populism). LR for \"the model prints 2.95%\" | (truth >= 2.6%) vs (< 2.6%) would be ~1.3 on its face, but four disclosed structural facts cut it: the model's China prints 2.85-4.58% and does not implement the locked -2.7pp/yr aging drag (its china_growth_contribution_pp is 1.41 against my 0.90); the production function is biophysical with gamma=0.55 on useful energy, so its own electrification build mechanically manufactures growth, which a Jones/Baumol task-complementarity view denies; it has no price level, no unemployment and no failable demand side, so the CQ6-D cell is structurally unrepresentable and the distribution is truncated from below; and its OECD-38 proxy (us + oecd-ex-us) omits Japan, Korea, Turkiye, Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Costa Rica, i.e. two of the three most-aged OECD economies, biasing the proxy up. Every reading is also low-fertility-conditional (UN WPP low variant), and over THIS window that conditioning is growth-FLATTERING, since low post-2025 fertility does not touch the 2032-36 working-age cohort but does lighten young-dependency. Effective LR 1.1. DISCOUNTED. Worth +0.05pp on P50, zero on the band.\n(4) Private-credit deterioration: Fitch US private-credit default rate 9.5% in July 2026 after 9.4% in June and 6.9% in July 2025, described as a record; median non-accruals at the 20 largest public BDCs 2.8% of cost in Q2 2026 against 2.0% at end-March (SOLVE via FT). Caveat preserved: Octus puts aggregate BDC non-accruals at $9.5B at cost, 1.9% of reported debt investments, DOWN 5% from $10B in Q1 2026, and KBRA reads the data as a normalisation of credit conditions rather than broad-based deterioration. The vendor series conflict. LR about 1.4, BELOW 2:1, DISCOUNTED. Worth a modest left-tail fattening only.\nBAND: P10 0.75 (recession inside the window plus a Chinese sub-2% outcome; a five-year average smooths, so 0.75 needs two bad years). P90 2.65, cut from the Historian's 2.9 by signal (2): the right tail requires China holding ~4.0% and the OECD reaching ~2.0% (0.665 x 2.0 + 0.335 x 4.0 = 2.67), and energizing the capacity that an OECD 2.0% implies is exactly what the lead-time evidence forecloses.\nDIRECTION: MODERATED downward, P50 -0.15pp off the Historian prior, with the band NARROWED by 0.25pp at the top. Signals (1) and (3) push up and are both discounted below 2:1; signal (2) pushes down and is the only one I can justify above it.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.382Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% year-on-year for four consecutive quarters. This directly tests the composition argument (Turkey, Mexico, Colombia, Chile, Costa Rica holding the aggregate above the G7 core) that is the load-bearing non-AI assumption in my whole payload.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 falls from 40 to about 24 (conjunct A from 0.72 to roughly 0.45). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 26 down to about 15, D from 14 up to about 24, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix the regime probabilities shift mass from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional holding to one of my best. This is the single cheapest observation that falsifies me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"FRED DFII10 (US 10y TIPS) 12-month moving average falls below 1.20% at any point, i.e. roughly 140bp below the locked 30 Oct 2026 print of 2.60%, without an accompanying MSCI World drawdown of >= 25% (so it is a level shift, not a flight-to-quality spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ4 P50 falls from 2.05 to about 1.50 and P10 from 1.10 to about 0.55, restoring the Historian's Reinhart-Sbrancia repression tail that my 3:1 likelihood ratio on the TIPS anchor currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 22 to about 15 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. In the asset matrix G7 long nominals and TIPS improve across all four regimes and my 2026-2030 buildout numbers for cash and ILBs are too high.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks, and gas turbine lead times below 36 months, on OEM order-book disclosures or NERC/EIA reporting (against >160 weeks and five years respectively as of Q1 2026).\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The 2.5:1 likelihood ratio that does most of the downward work in my CQ3 collapses. CQ3 P50 rises from 1.75 to about 1.95 and P90 from 2.65 back to about 2.90; CQ6-A rises from 11 to about 16-17 and B falls correspondingly. It also raises realized AI-Q3 datacentre load back toward the locked 998 TWh P50 and cuts my upward correction to AI-Q4. Power/grid and construction conditional returns fall (the scarcity rent disappears), AI-complex conditional returns rise.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS monthly release) 12-month average exceeds 2.0% for four consecutive quarters, with core ex-food-and-energy above 1.8% — i.e. the PPI +5.0% signal of Sep-2026 transmits to the household price level rather than decoupling as it did in 2021.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 conjunct B (0.62) is wrong and CQ2 falls from 40 to about 22. It also means the Chinese demand deficit I have priced is resolving, which raises my China growth leg above 2.8%, lifts CQ3 P50 by roughly 0.10-0.15pp through the 0.335 PPP weight, and improves China equities across every regime while worsening onshore duration.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey or the OECD earnings database shows ISIC F+Q real median hourly wage growth leading J+K+M by >= 3.0 percentage points cumulative since 2025 (i.e. on pace for the +10pp gate), rather than the dead heat the Atlanta Fed tracker currently shows at 4.2 against 4.2.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ1 rises from 9 to about 25 (conjunct C2 from 0.15 to roughly 0.45), and the Stanford/ADP hiring-volume-not-pay mechanism that is my only above-2:1 signal on that question is falsified. It also means physical scarcity is clearing by PRICE rather than by rationing, which raises CQ6-C from 26 toward 32 through the cost-push channel and makes construction/trades and healthcare operators materially better holdings than I have priced in regimes B and C.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'growth'."}}